\[g=\frac{7}{8}\;g_{\rm q}+g_{\rm g}, \tag{1.2}\]

where \(g_{\rm q}\) and \(g_{\rm g}\) are the degeneracies of, respectively, the quark and gluon states. Each quark flavor has a quark/antiquark state, two spin states and three color states whereas each gluon has two spin states and eight color states. The total degeneracy is therefore given by

\[g=\frac{7}{8}\times n_{\rm f}\times 2\times 2\times 3+2\times 8=\frac{21}{2}n_{ \rm f}+16, \tag{1.3}\]

which yields the value \(g=37(95/2)\) for an \(n_{\rm f}=2(3)\) flavor QGP. This is an order of magnitude larger than for a hadron gas where \(g\approx 3\).

