### 7.1 Cross section

The invariant differential cross section of \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) production in inelastic \(p+p\) interactions is given by

\[E\frac{\mathrm{d}^{3}\sigma_{\mathrm{inel}}^{p+p}}{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{p}^{3}}=E\frac{ \mathrm{d}^{3}\sigma_{\mathrm{NSD}}^{p+p}}{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{p}^{3}}=\sigma_{ \mathrm{NSD}}^{p+p}\;\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}N}{2\pi p_{T}\,\mathrm{d}p_{T}\, \mathrm{d}y}. \tag{7.1}\]

It has been shown that the singly diffractive (SD) contribution to the inelastic cross section is negligible at \(p_{T}>1\,\mathrm{GeV}\!/c\)[77] so that we can assume that the differential inelastic cross section is equal to the differential NSD cross section in our \(p_{T}\) range. The total NSD cross section in \(p+p\) collisions was taken to be \(\sigma_{\mathrm{NSD}}^{p+p}=30.0\pm 3.5\,\mathrm{mb}\), as described in Section 4.1. The total hadronic cross section in \(d+\mathrm{Au}\) collisions was taken to be \(\sigma_{\mathrm{hadr}}^{d+\mathrm{Au}}=2.21\pm 0.09\,\mathrm{b}\)[15].

In tables 7.1 and 7.2 we list the cross sections calculated from Eq. (7.1) for the \(p+p\) and \(d+\mathrm{Au}\) datasets. In the third column of these tables are given the statistical errors while in the remaining columns the quadratic sum of the systematic errors are given separately for each group defined in Table 6.1. In addition to these \(p_{T}\)-dependent systematic errors the quadratic sum of the normalization uncertainties is found to be \(12.2\%\) for the \(p+p\) and \(5.6\%\) for the \(d+\mathrm{Au}\) data.

